# Ban Unjustified Opens

This rule bans unqualified deep-reflection access into a Java module. A bare `opens a.b.c;` (or an `open module`) grants *every* other module reflective access to a package's private members, which defeats strong encapsulation. A framework that needs reflective access should be named explicitly with a qualified `opens a.b.c to some.framework;`.

Packages that genuinely must be opened to the whole world can be listed in `allowedOpens` to pass the check. The rule does nothing for non-modular projects (no `module-info.class`).

## Choosing the phase

This rule inspects the compiled `module-info.class` under `\${project.build.outputDirectory}`, which exists only once the project has been built. Bind the `enforce` execution to an appropriate phase: the default `validate` phase runs before any class is compiled, so an execution left there finds nothing and reports success without having checked anything. For an ordinary project `process-classes` is the earliest phase that works.

Where the descriptor is *injected into the packaged artifact* after compilation -- as [Moditect](https://github.com/moditect/moditect) does -- the output directory never holds one and this rule cannot inspect it at any phase. Use [requireExplicitModules](./requireExplicitModules.html), which falls back to the packaged artifact, to assert that such a project ships a named module at all.

When the rule finds nothing at all to inspect it emits a warning naming this page, so a misbound execution cannot pass unnoticed.

Both output layouts are supported: the classic one (the descriptor directly in the output directory, one Maven project = one Java module) and the Maven 4 *module source hierarchy* (POM model 4.1.0), where one Maven project compiles several modules, each to its own subdirectory `\${project.build.outputDirectory}/<module-name>/`.

The following parameters are supported by this rule:

- **allowedOpens** - a list of package names that are allowed to be opened unqualifiedly despite the ban.

- **message** - an optional message to the user if the rule fails.

Sample Plugin Configuration:

```xml
<project>
  [...]
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>ban-unjustified-opens</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>enforce</goal>
            </goals>
            <phase>process-classes</phase>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <banUnjustifiedOpens>
                  <allowedOpens>
                    <allowedOpen>com.example.foo.dto</allowedOpen>
                  </allowedOpens>
                </banUnjustifiedOpens>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  [...]
</project>
```
